From: fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)
Date: Sun, 28 Aug 1994 22:58:14 -0500
Subject: [#94-8-286] PSYCH: Blocking the Blue

Clip-on sunglasses are very nerdy, right? So it was only appropriate
that I should pick up a pair for driving. I happened to seize upon a
US$6 pair of Blu-blocker(tm) clones, and was in for an interesting
experience.

First off, the yellow-orange lenses do seem to strip out a lot of
the blue end of the spectrum. Grass lawns look bright green like
Astroturf(tm :-) and dark blue signs, cars, etc. look black. The most
amusing shift so far -- Huntsville DOT trucks, which match the color of
a Butterfinger wrapper under full-spectrum light, are nursery pink
with my glasses on.

More interesting was the validation of one of the infomercial claims --
they do seem to cut out haze, enhance binocular vision, and make it
easier to resolve certain details. Makes me eager for a set of
programmable 1 mm - to - 1 nm glasses, once they're not too heavy :-)
(Of course a lot of the longer end of that range is of limited use at
the bottom of an atmospheric miasma, but most of us would agree on
the solution to that, also. It might be fun to extend out to about a 
meter, at least I could scrap my radar detector).

While my cortex was revelling in the revelations, though, the upfront
parts of my visual system were not happy campers. I felt a definite
discomfort around and behind the eyes as my retinas and optic nerves
tried this, tried that, and tried the other, to get recalibrated. This
went on for about two hours over the course of three days, and seems
to have subsided, as if my visual system had learned the new mode. This 
leads me to wonder how many new spectra the existing equipment could 
learn, and worry a little that the process might be damaging in some
subtle way.

