Not unless they can be better formulated. I don't know anyone who wears the
same color glasses all the time, except maybe those with tinted contacts; and
how would you expect them to differ, anyway? And how measure it?

> At the risk of appearing
>Ludditish, "its not nice to fool Mother Nature."  Try a pair of Ott's
>glasses and see how you feel. Here's looking at you. 

I haven't run across any scholarly work on the subject; I am aware of
some probably-pseudo-science about full-spectrum lights (which were quite 
expensive when I looked at them), and I know my wife firmly believes that
warm yellow lights and/or yellow glasses help SAD (Seasonal Affective
Disorder) sufferers during winter or long cloudy spells.

I seriously doubt that a human is critically sensitive to the exact spectrum
of light the same way a (green) plant might be, since the plant's nutrition
depends on the light, and the plant has evolved various pigmented enzymes in
certain proportions. OTOH, I'd have to know more about the controls on that
plants-under-glass study. What does plain glass take out besides UV, anyway?
How about temperature and moisture? etc.

And, lastly, we "fool Mother Nature" all the time. I fool her into thinking I'm
one of the top predators on this planet by carrying a gun; into thinking I have
20/20 vision by wearing glasses (I hear no one warning me that my eyes need
their full natural dose of UV!); and so on.

Oh, and while we're designing my optical enhancers, we of course want zoom
lenses, amplifiers, spectral analyzers, and record/playback. And someone making
movies to be appreciated by Boundlessly-Self-Transformed-Visionaries.
